#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163048} :activity/id #uuid "af1642f1-edfe-5e8b-bb3e-376bc11c5af4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050205 :topic/id #uuid "36128875-8025-5e37-8397-54d8247d47c9" :topic/math-academy-id 3131} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163048 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163315 :tutorial/content "Let's suppose we are given the matrix\n\n![](images/18/18f57c05cb79918e17401427e3419648791562a5d38116582e0d396e20db1069.svg)\n\nThen, the corresponding linear transformation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf x \\mapsto A \\mathbf x\n$$\n\nmaps the unit circle $\\big\\{ \\mathbf x \\, | \\, \\|\\mathbf x\\|=1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb R^2$ onto an ellipse $\\big\\{ A\\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/c1/c163f7714d0e2045db37438b7ea66a2dc8ac92cfbe309a0d24c898bbfac8c295.png)\n\n \n\nHow can we find the maximum possible value of $\\|A \\mathbf x\\|?$ Geometrically, this is equivalent to finding the semi-major axis of our ellipse.\n\n![](images/86/866052291b0d7a416988c0c42fa99eec1d1bfd3b1344cf3cf2eb0a09457ae38d.png)\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n- The value of $\\mathbf x$ that maximizes $||A\\mathbf x||$ also maximizes $||A\\mathbf x||^2.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf v$ is a column vector, then $||\\mathbf v||^2 = \\mathbf v^T \\mathbf v\\mathbin{:}$\n\nTherefore, we can re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNote that $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is a quadratic form with the matrix $A^T A.$ Therefore, the maximum value of this quadratic form for $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1$ equals the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^TA\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}10-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (10-\\lambda )^{2}-36 & =0 \\\\ (10-\\lambda )^{2} & =36 \\\\ 10-\\lambda  & =\\pm 6 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ is $16,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 16\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\} = \\sqrt{16} = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the length of the semi-major axis of our ellipse equals $4.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b6f73158-b78c-5262-bb9d-a957e3f79fa3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6133 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "62510b93-1090-5902-b6a0-45fc7a644d01" :step/math-academy-id 37249 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163049}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162097 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161910 :question/id #uuid "f4a3741e-a87c-5b57-95b3-5ef47f7ad599" :question/math-academy-id "e-9907"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d116374b-b693-5d50-8019-fcc5a7766b9b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050447}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493930}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere"} :step/id #uuid "01d42207-c2b3-55a2-9d11-6fcda35c9cca" :step/math-academy-id 37250 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163052}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162098 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161911 :question/id #uuid "06ca6e76-6354-5450-882e-b0c063acd303" :question/math-academy-id "e-9908"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cec3c21-28df-52ba-9b78-86cffb4c7720" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400770}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Regarding the Image of the Unit Eigenvectors of a Quadratic Form"} :step/id #uuid "bc576dbc-6ac4-5a02-9348-f4cf6db1eb0c" :step/math-academy-id 37251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162099 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161912 :question/id #uuid "23c1671b-da2c-570b-af4f-c6aad258155d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9909"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503755}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"} :step/id #uuid "94acc5c3-ff1a-5747-b32d-b0db6e46436e" :step/math-academy-id 37254 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163050}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163052 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163316 :tutorial/content "Let $A$ be an $m\\times n$ matrix. Then, $A^T A$ is always a symmetric, positive semi-definite matrix. In particular, $A^TA$ can be orthogonally diagonalized, and its eigenvalues are non-negative real numbers.\n\nThe **singular values** of  $A$ are defined as the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix $A^T A.$ The $i$th singular value of $A$ is denoted $\\sigma_i\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- $\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}$\n\n- $\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2}$\n\n- $\\sigma_3 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_3}$\n\n$\\:\\vdots$\n\n- $\\sigma_n = \\sqrt{\\lambda_n}$\n\nWe usually list the singular values in *decreasing* order. \n\nNow, we know that $A^TA$ is orthogonally diagonalizable. Therefore, if\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2\\,\\ldots, \\mathbf v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nis an *orthonormal* basis of $\\mathbb R^n$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A^T}{A}$ with the corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1\\geq \\lambda_2\\geq \\ldots \\geq \\lambda_n,\n$$\n\nthen it can be shown that\n\n- $\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_1,$\n\n- $\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_2,$\n\n$\\:\\vdots$\n\n- $\\sigma_n = \\sqrt{\\lambda_n}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_n.$\n\nFurthermore:\n\n- $\\sigma_1$ is the maximum value of $||A\\mathbf x||$ over all *unit* vectors $\\mathbf x,$ and is attained on the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf v_1$\n\n- $\\sigma_2$ is the maximum value of $||A\\mathbf x||$ over all *unit* vectors $\\mathbf x$ that are orthogonal to $\\mathbf v_1,$ and is attained on the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf v_2$\n\n- $\\sigma_3$ is the maximum value of $||A\\mathbf x||$ over all *unit* vectors $\\mathbf x$ that are orthogonal to $\\mathbf v_1$ *and* $\\mathbf v_2,$ and is attained on the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf v_3$\n\n$\\:\\vdots$\n\n- $\\sigma_n$ is the maximum value of $||A\\mathbf x||$ over all *unit* vectors $\\mathbf x$ that are orthogonal to $\\mathbf v_1,\\mathbf v_2,\\ldots,\\mathbf v_{n-1},$ and is attained on the unit eigenvector $\\mathbf v_n$\n\nLet's take a look at a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "f470bdb6-fa79-595c-beba-f8242d4426bd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6134 :tutorial/title "The Singular Values of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "ef1b9251-7972-5caa-8ac8-321830fa4c27" :step/math-academy-id 37255 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163056}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163056 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163320 :tutorial/content "We've seen that for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/9c/9c11e176876a767543250d5b18038cae7b14a36a692a6f14474da6b4312a48b9.svg)\n\nthe eigenvalues of $A^TA$ are $\\lambda_1=16$ and $\\lambda_2=4.$ It's easy to show that the corresponding unit eigenvectors of $A^T A$ are \n\n![](images/7c/7c44b67b766ea8385f4d7780523ee560bd3e215a0cee42db9baf208da471ccdd.svg) \n\nThe singular values of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{16}=4, \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting all of this information together gives us the following diagram. \n\n![](images/23/238f4ae60c783dd786476a339f012ef1b4ca82bcac7aa3f3ef4445726938ef3a.png)\n\n \n\nNote that our ellipse's semi-major and semi-minor axes are $\\sigma_1 = 4$ and $\\sigma_2 =2,$ respectively." :tutorial/id #uuid "a319c696-fd06-5e8f-99f1-26ad08271a27" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6139 :tutorial/title "Associating Singular Values With an Ellipse's Principal Axes"} :step/id #uuid "c9015782-c2cb-5d34-8e6d-84a7dd5678d9" :step/math-academy-id 37280 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163051}}] :activity/title "The Singular Values of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162225}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503786} :activity/id #uuid "d2500c5e-c96c-5026-89b9-c0eff4d22db5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050205 :topic/id #uuid "36128875-8025-5e37-8397-54d8247d47c9" :topic/math-academy-id 3131} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503778 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "371aa83b-7675-5a37-b450-ceb326f383dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266620} {:answer/id #uuid "3b20f717-34f8-50ab-b29d-bd468f139b4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266666} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb66304-70cb-53f0-b508-f2f4cfc15286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267415} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4e98f1-3aab-5869-8b67-d9a7f948bf2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267582} {:answer/id #uuid "e3a2dd40-7b9e-5445-928c-b6bb8a1dc03d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268153}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266666} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebf581fc-936a-577e-8513-af09419c38fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0157c09c-4c34-5a09-a678-edce758d03a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-139543" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=12,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=9,$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=12,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={12}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={12}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=12.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=12.$"} :step/id #uuid "410ad0cd-66aa-589e-9d24-b7c90f23a34c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503797}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503786 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27612a52-9a44-5194-990a-b67bd5b0cb3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266495} {:answer/id #uuid "3781afe7-ca23-5caf-8d77-e210ce87765d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266623} {:answer/id #uuid "9cbfef3a-23de-5494-b267-533971519fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267552} {:answer/id #uuid "a48b2a55-e19f-5a3b-aad5-9311f9e9ad3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267628} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e9b4e6-ef6f-5008-83b9-4dc476b26261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7{v}_{1}+5{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267677}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267628} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f73eb28-9edf-5454-bbc8-1e8090f60bd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "14414eae-18ed-5455-a3d4-1f9724606970" :question/math-academy-id "q-139421" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f26e876d5234313926a2cbaa8938e32c8a725cf3435ce3113361bfa3dc1b7997.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $25$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=7={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={7}^{2}=49,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={5}^{2}=25.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=25$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "52f281c4-dfee-54b8-b75f-07ea8b7aff89" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503804}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503797 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "220208ba-fab8-55a0-8a2f-2eb8fb671d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388620} {:answer/id #uuid "5dde93b7-ebe5-5d5f-8618-0c649d764704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388621} {:answer/id #uuid "d8598738-9382-5b1a-b55a-73d18649f6d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388622} {:answer/id #uuid "f6853d60-cd68-56ae-8530-5d5610a55167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388623} {:answer/id #uuid "fce765ef-ed14-5c1c-a75f-4c9e2f5446a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "A{A}^{T}{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388624}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388621} :answer-field/id #uuid "e12f6b66-e0b5-5c3d-87c1-2792f81acfc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "82b609f1-ebbe-5986-a6ff-a556a13681d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-139549" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7a2aea092b82dd217b848abdfc162425f098bc13c53975ccfced3f60a4e2ebfb.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $16$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=8={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={8}^{2}=64,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=4={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={4}^{2}=16.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=16$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "74ddedfd-6201-5f03-9796-484a0235a391"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503801 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c5df5ba-75f0-5bfc-a30e-6793337660ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503195} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6dfca0-cb60-5c5b-ae49-7cd685619f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503239} {:answer/id #uuid "87db152d-bf36-549b-bb17-dd5daccc80b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503259} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b73526-0075-5162-b576-f6193e008526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503320} {:answer/id #uuid "f92b14f4-9494-5bbe-b406-714b16837483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503239} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e35f8d5-2126-585d-9e2b-f8a2c67ed434" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f2c3caea-6032-5d20-9fea-52c78472efb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-139538" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=8$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3\\sqrt{3},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=8,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={8}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={8}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=8.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=8.$"} :step/id #uuid "80e7be25-cdff-5c16-856f-410a8631d1d8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503778}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a429996-164b-54fc-9946-577431de1ccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266657} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3a6a58-573b-5732-bf74-6c0a1ac7d701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266849} {:answer/id #uuid "62be0fb0-0dcb-57b3-9f91-1d3ab410a49b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267012} {:answer/id #uuid "afc186cf-aab9-589d-af44-efe97c49758b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267725} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7691e1-a3bb-53c8-9a1f-15a33a8deed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267981}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266657} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a4b6387-6d51-5a91-bc9a-e1d31b9ff78c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ed90c3b2-491c-5a1e-a27e-a0b50d59ad00" :question/math-academy-id "q-139535" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{3},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=9,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={9}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={9}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=9.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=9.$"} :step/id #uuid "85e42921-05f4-5ace-8862-09474ca442a5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503801}}] :activity/title "The Singular Values of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502976}]}
